6"><span class="enc-name">Ala ad-Din Muhammad II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala ad-Din Muhammad II was the shah of the Khwarazmian Empire from 1200 to 1220.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-tekish"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-tekish.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala ad-Din Tekish</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala ad-Din Tekish was the ruler of Khwarazm from 1172 to 1200.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-bozkurt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-bozkurt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt was bey of the Dulkadirids, the Turkmen principality around Elbistan and Maraş, from 1480 to 1515.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-muhammad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-muhammad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad, known as Ibn Kakuya, was the founder of the Kakuyid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-ali"><span class="enc-name">Ala al-Din Ali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala al-Din Ali was the last effective ruler of the Eretnid sultanate of central Anatolia, grandson of its founder Eretna.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-husayn"><span class="enc-name">Ala al-Din Husayn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala al-Din Husayn was ruler of Ghor from about 1149 to 1161.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-ala-ibn-mughith"><span class="enc-name">Al-Ala ibn Mughith</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Ala ibn Mughith was an Arab leader in al-Andalus who in 763 raised the black banner of the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur against the Umayyad emir Abd al-Rahman I…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala Singh was a Sikh chief of the Phulkian family and the founder of Patiala State.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-ahmad-shah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-ahmad-shah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ala-ud-Din Ahmad Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala-ud-Din Ahmad Shah was a sultan of the Bahmani Sultanate in the Deccan, reigning from 1436 to 1458 at Bidar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-bahman-shah"><span class="enc-name">Ala-ud-Din Bahman Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ala-ud-Din Bahman Shah, born Hasan, was the founder of the Bahmani Sultanate in the Deccan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaafin"><span class="enc-name">Alaafin</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alaafin was the king of the Oyo Empire, a sacred ruler regarded as the embodiment of divine Yoruba kingship.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alabama"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alabama.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alabama</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Alabama is a state in the south-eastern United States, stretching from the Tennessee River valley to the Gulf of Mexico at Mobile.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-bey"><span class="enc-name">Alaeddin Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alaeddin Bey was ruler of the Karamanids in central Anatolia from about 1361.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alaeddin Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alaeddin Mosque is the main mosque of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum in Konya, Turkey, standing on Alaeddin Hill at the heart of the city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alagakkonara-family"><span class="enc-name">Alagakkonara family</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alagakkonara, or Alakeshvara, were a powerful family of ministers in 14th- and early 15th-century Sri Lanka, of south Indian origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alahis"><span class="enc-name">Alahis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alahis was a Lombard Duke of Trent, and later of Brescia, who rebelled against King Cunipert.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alai-darwaza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alai-darwaza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alai Darwaza</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alai Darwaza is a domed gateway in the Qutb Minar complex in Delhi, built in 1311 by Alauddin Khalji as the southern entrance to the Quwwat-ul-Islam mosque.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alain-bouchart"><span class="enc-name">Alain Bouchart</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alain Bouchart was a Breton lawyer and chronicler who served Duke Francis II and Duchess Anne of Brittany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alake-of-egbaland"><span class="enc-name">Alake of Egbaland</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alake of Egbaland is the paramount traditional ruler of the Egba, a Yoruba people whose capital is Abeokuta in south-western Nigeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaknanda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alaknanda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alaknanda</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alaknanda is a Himalayan river in Uttarakhand, India, and one of the two headstreams of the Ganges.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaksandu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alaksandu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alaksandu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alaksandu was a king of Wilusa in north-western Anatolia in the early 13th century BCE, known from a treaty he made with the Hittite king Muwatalli II around 1280 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alalakh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alalakh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alalakh</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Alalakh is a Bronze Age city mound at Tell Atchana near Antakya in southern Turkey, in the Amuq plain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alalia"><span class="enc-name">Alalia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Alalia was a Greek colony on the east coast of Corsica, founded by Phocaeans around 565 BCE, on the site of modern Aléria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alam-khan-lodi"><span class="enc-name">Alam Khan Lodi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alam Khan Lodi was a son of Bahlul Lodi and uncle of Ibrahim Lodi, the last sultan of Delhi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alamgir-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alamgir-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alamgir II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alamgir II was Mughal emperor from 1754 to 1759, a son of Jahandar Shah placed on the throne by the vizier Ghazi-ud-din Khan Imad-ul-Mulk.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alamo-mission"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alamo-mission.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alamo Mission</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Alamo Mission, founded in 1718 as Mission San Antonio de Valero, was a Franciscan mission at San Antonio in Spanish Texas that gathered Coahuiltecan peoples.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alamut"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alamut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alamut</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Alamut is a mountain fortress in the Alborz range of northern Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/alan-garcia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/alan-garcia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Alan García</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Alan García was a Peruvian politician of APRA who was president of Peru from 1985 to 1990 and from 2006 to 2011.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/alan-i-king-of-brittany"><span class="enc-name">Alan I, King of Brittany</span><span clas